course: french
lesson: 39
title: Olympiad: decoding a short French text ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In a language olympiad you often get a short real text - a postcard, a sign, a menu, a recipe - and must work out what it says without a dictionary, like a code-breaker.
explain: Start with the words you know (famille, chat, trois, fromage), then the lookalikes that really are friends (perché, perched; sucre, sugar), and only then guess the rest from what makes sense in that place.
explain: Watch for traps: false friends like librairie, tiny words that flip the meaning like ne ... pas, and French habits such as a comma in a price - 9,50 € means nine euros fifty.
explain: Picture a lamppost in Dieppe with a poster: "Perdu : petit chat noir, yeux verts." The first word is the key - perdu means lost - so this is someone looking for their cat, not selling one.
explain: Then check your answer against the whole text: if your meaning does not fit a sign in a park or a note on a fridge, look again.
fact: Léa's postcard - "Je suis à Nice avec ma famille. Il fait beau et chaud. Hier, on a nagé dans la mer." - means she is in Nice with her family, the weather is fine and hot, and yesterday they swam in the sea; la mer is the sea and la mère is the mother.
fact: "Maître Corbeau, sur un arbre perché, tenait en son bec un fromage" is the start of a fable by La Fontaine (1668): Mr Crow, perched in a tree, held a cheese in his beak; a fox later tricks him into dropping it.
fact: "Menu enfant : steak haché, frites, glace à la vanille - 9,50 €" is a kids' menu: a minced-beef patty, chips and vanilla ice cream for 9 euros 50; French writes a comma where English writes a decimal point.
fact: "Défense de marcher sur la pelouse" means "Keep off the grass": défense de means it is forbidden to, marcher is to walk, la pelouse is the lawn.
fact: "Je suis un fruit jaune. Les singes m'adorent. Qui suis-je ?" - I am a yellow fruit, monkeys love me, who am I? The answer is une banane; les singes are monkeys.
fact: "Demain, à Paris, il pleut le matin, mais l'après-midi il y a du soleil. Il fait dix-huit degrés." means rain in the morning, sun in the afternoon, 18 degrees.
fact: "De la farine, trois œufs, du lait et un peu de sucre" means flour, three eggs, milk and a little sugar.
fact: "Perdu : petit chat noir, yeux verts. Il s'appelle Minou." means "Lost: small black cat with green eyes, called Minou".
fact: Départ means departure and arrivée means arrival; a train leaving at 14h05 and arriving at 16h50 takes 2 hours 45 minutes.
fact: "Samedi, je vais à la librairie avec mon père. J'achète un livre sur les chevaux." means on Saturday she goes to the bookshop with her dad and buys a book about horses.
fact: "Le goûter est dans le placard. Ne mangez pas tout le chocolat !" means the after-school snack is in the cupboard - don't eat all the chocolate; ne ... pas makes it "don't".
exercise: Léa writes "Je suis à Nice avec ma famille." Where is she? | In Nice, with her family | In Paris, with her friends | At home in Nice, alone | In Nice, at her school
exercise: Léa writes "Il fait beau et chaud." What is the weather? | Fine and hot | Cold and wet | Windy and cold | Cloudy and warm
exercise: Léa writes "Hier, on a nagé dans la mer." What did they do yesterday? | They swam in the sea | They swam with her mother | They sailed on the sea | They walked by the sea
exercise: In the fable, what is Maître Corbeau? | Mr Crow, a bird | A master cook | A schoolteacher | A king of the forest
exercise: Where is the crow, and what does he hold? | In a tree, with a cheese in his beak | In a nest, with an egg in his claws | On a roof, with bread in his beak | In a tree, with a fish in his claws
exercise: What is steak haché on a French menu? | A minced-beef patty | A big chopped salad | A fried fish steak | A chicken sandwich
exercise: How much is the kids' menu that says 9,50 €? | Nine euros fifty | Nine hundred and fifty euros | Ninety-five euros | Fifty-nine euros
exercise: A park sign says "Défense de marcher sur la pelouse." What does it mean? | Keep off the grass | The army marches here | Defend the lawn | March on the grass
exercise: "Je suis un fruit jaune. Les singes m'adorent." Who am I? | une banane | un citron | une pomme | une orange
exercise: What are les singes? | Monkeys | Singers | Birds | Bananas
exercise: The forecast says "il pleut le matin". Does Kai need an umbrella for the morning? | Yes, it rains in the morning | No, it's sunny all morning | Only in the afternoon | No, it's snowing instead
exercise: What temperature is "dix-huit degrés"? | 18 degrees | 10 degrees | 8 degrees | 28 degrees
exercise: The recipe says "de la farine, trois œufs, du lait et un peu de sucre." What is farine? | Flour | A farm | Butter | Salt
exercise: How many eggs does the crêpe recipe need? | Three | Two | One | A little
exercise: A poster says "Perdu : petit chat noir, yeux verts." What is it? | A lost-cat poster | A cat for sale | A pet show advert | A cat-food advert
exercise: A train leaves at 14h05 and arrives at 16h50. How long is the journey? | 2 hours 45 minutes | 2 hours 55 minutes | 3 hours 45 minutes | 2 hours 15 minutes
exercise: "Samedi, je vais à la librairie... J'achète un livre." Where is she going? | To a bookshop, to buy a book | To a library, to borrow a book | To a bakery, to buy bread | To school, to read a book
exercise: The fridge note says "Ne mangez pas tout le chocolat !" What does Mum mean? | Don't eat all the chocolate | Eat all the chocolate | There's no chocolate left | Buy some more chocolate
test: ★★★ Emma from Leeds reads "on a nagé dans la mer" and goes, "Easy, they swam with their mum, like." What's the trap? | La mer is the sea; mother is la mère | Nagé means walked, not swam | On a means they will, not they did | Nothing, la mer means mum
test: ★★★ Leo from Dublin asks, "Who's the master, and why's he got cheese in a tree?" Which retelling is right? | Mr Crow, in a tree, holds a cheese | A master chef is hiding cheese in a tree | A fox is up a tree eating cheese | A crow is fishing from a tree
test: ★★★ Olivia from Bath reads 9,50 € and gasps, "Nine hundred and fifty euros for chips?!" How much would two kids' menus really cost? | 19 euros | 1,900 euros | 18 euros 50 | 9 euros 50
test: ★★★ Jacob from Cardiff reads "Défense de marcher sur la pelouse" and goes, "Defence of marching? Is there an army, butt?" (butt = mate, in Wales) What does the sign really say? | Don't walk on the grass | Soldiers march on the lawn | Protect the marching band | Walk only on the lawn
test: ★★★ Ruby from Belfast doesn't know singes and answers un citron to the riddle. Why is that weak? | Monkeys love bananas, not lemons | Lemons are never yellow in France | Citron is the French word for banana | Nothing, citron is the right answer
test: ★★★ Kai from Auckland plans a picnic in Paris for the morning. The forecast says "il pleut le matin, mais l'après-midi il y a du soleil". What should he do? | Move the picnic to the afternoon | Keep the picnic in the morning | Cancel the whole day, it's snowing | Bring sun cream for the morning
test: ★★★ Mia from Ohio thinks farine is "a farm" and sucre is "a sucker". What does she actually need to buy? | Flour, 3 eggs, milk, a little sugar | A farm, three eggs, milk and a sweet | Flour, three eggs, butter and salt | Fruit, two eggs, milk and lots of sugar
test: ★★★ Harvey from Kent sees "Perdu : petit chat noir" on a lamppost and goes, "Bargain, a cat for sale!" What is it really, and what should he do if he sees the cat? | A lost cat; call the number on the poster | A cat for sale; ring and offer money | A cat show; buy a ticket | A free cat; take it home
test: ★★★ Josh from Leeds says, "14h05 to 16h50? Two hours fifty-five, sorted." Which way of counting is right? | 2 h to 16h05, then 45 min | Nowhere, it really is 2 h 55 | 3 h to 17h05, then 15 min more | 1 h to 15h05, then 45 min
test: ★★★ Sophie from Glasgow reads "je vais à la librairie... J'achète un livre sur les chevaux" and says, "She's borrowing a horse book from the library, aye?" Which word proves she's wrong? | j'achète, I'm buying | samedi, Saturday | avec, with | sur, about
test: ★★★ Tom's gran from Somerset reads the fridge note and goes, "Eat all the chocolate, my lover - Mum says so!" Which two little words did she miss? | ne and pas, which make it "don't" | le and la, which make it plural | tout and le, which make it some | est and dans, which make it there
exam: Ascertain the activity undertaken by the writer of the postcard on the preceding day, as stated in the phrase "Hier, on a nagé dans la mer". | Swimming in the sea | Swimming with her mother | Sailing upon the sea | Walking along the shore
exam: Identify the sum denoted by the inscription "9,50 €" upon a French menu. | Nine euros and fifty cents | Nine hundred and fifty euros | Ninety-five euros | Ninety-five cents
exam: Select the rendering most consistent with the notice "Défense de marcher sur la pelouse". | Walking upon the lawn is prohibited | The lawn is reserved for military exercises | The lawn is to be defended by the public | Marching upon the lawn is encouraged
exam: It is given that a train departs at 14h05 and arrives at 16h50. Determine the duration of the journey. | Two hours and forty-five minutes | Two hours and fifty-five minutes | Three hours and forty-five minutes | One hour and forty-five minutes
